Here’s a pre-upgrade checklist for enterprise apps. Adapt the depth to each application’s criticality.
1. Inventory & scope
	•	Current version, edition, patch/CU level for every app
	•	Vendor support status (in-support, EOL, extended support)
	•	Upgrade path (direct vs stepped — many products force interim versions)
	•	Owners, stakeholders, and business criticality tier per app
	•	Dependency map: upstream feeders, downstream consumers, shared services
2. Compatibility matrix
	•	OS version (current + target) — check vendor’s supported matrix, not just “it runs”
	•	Database engine and edition (e.g. SQL Server version, compat level, collation)
	•	Runtimes: .NET, Java JDK, Python, Node — exact versions
	•	Middleware: IIS, web servers, message brokers, identity providers (ADFS/Entra)
	•	Client/browser support if web-based
	•	Integration partners’ API/contract versions
	•	Licensing model changes (per-core vs per-user, subscription shifts)
3. Release intelligence
	•	Read full release notes between current → target version (not just latest)
	•	Catalog breaking changes, deprecated features, removed APIs
	•	Known issues / open bugs against target version
	•	Required prerequisite patches on OS/DB before upgrade
4. Backups & rollback
	•	Full DB backup + verified restore test
	•	App config, certificates, keys, service account credentials exported
	•	VM snapshots or image-level backup where applicable
	•	Documented, tested rollback procedure (untested = doesn’t exist)
	•	Rollback decision criteria and time budget defined
5. Environment & data
	•	Dev/test/UAT/prod parity confirmed
	•	Baseline captured: performance metrics, query plans, response times, resource use
	•	Storage headroom on data, log, tempdb, backup volumes
	•	HA/DR implications — for SQL Server AGs: secondary upgrade order, listener behaviour, quorum
	•	Certificate expiry dates checked (upgrades often surface latent cert issues)
6. Security & compliance
	•	Service accounts: permissions still valid post-upgrade, no deprecated auth modes
	•	TLS/cipher requirements of new version
	•	Audit logging continuity (schema changes can break SIEM ingestion)
	•	Compliance impact: GDPR, ISO 27001, PCI, SOC2 — re-attestation needed?
	•	Penetration test / vulnerability scan on target version
	•	Data residency unchanged
7. Testing plan
	•	Smoke test scripts ready
	•	Regression suite covering critical paths
	•	Integration tests with every upstream/downstream system
	•	Performance test against captured baseline
	•	UAT signoff criteria and test users provisioned
	•	Failover/failback test for HA configurations
8. Operational readiness
	•	Maintenance window agreed, CAB approval secured
	•	Runbooks updated with exact commands and expected outputs
	•	Monitoring thresholds reviewed (new version may have different normal)
	•	Alerting paths tested
	•	Support team briefed on changes and known issues
	•	Vendor support case opened proactively for high-risk upgrades
9. Communications
	•	Downtime notification sent (with timing buffer)
	•	Status page / comms channel during the change
	•	Escalation contacts (internal + vendor) on standby
	•	Post-upgrade announcement template ready
10. Post-upgrade verification
	•	Smoke + critical-path tests pass
	•	Performance within X% of baseline (define X up front)
	•	Error logs reviewed for new warnings/errors
	•	Backups taken and verified on upgraded system
	•	Monitoring confirms stable for N hours before sign-off
	•	Documentation, CMDB, and architecture diagrams updated.​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​